    "['no shutdown', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 disable false', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 disable false\\nset interfaces ge-2/0/0 disable false\\n##\\n\\nAlternatively, if using the operational mode command (not configuration mode), it would be:\\n##\\nactivate interfaces ge-1/0/0\\nactivate interfaces ge-2/0/0\\n##\\n\\nNote: The Juniper equivalent of Cisco\\'s \"no shutdown\" is to set the interface\\'s disable parameter to \"false\" in configuration mode, or use the \"activate\" command in operational mode. I\\'ve provided both versions for clarity, but the configuration mode version (set disable false) is the direct counterpart to Cisco\\'s configuration command.', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 disable false'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-2/0/0 disable false', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 disable false\\nset interfaces ge-2/0/0 disable false\\n##\\n\\nAlternatively, if using the operational mode command (not configuration mode), it would be:\\n##\\nactivate interfaces ge-1/0/0\\nactivate interfaces ge-2/0/0\\n##\\n\\nNote: The Juniper equivalent of Cisco\\'s \"no shutdown\" is to set the interface\\'s disable parameter to \"false\" in configuration mode, or use the \"activate\" command in operational mode. I\\'ve provided both versions for clarity, but the configuration mode version (set disable false) is the direct counterpart to Cisco\\'s configuration command.', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-2/0/0 disable false'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'activate interfaces ge-1/0/0', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 disable false\\nset interfaces ge-2/0/0 disable false\\n##\\n\\nAlternatively, if using the operational mode command (not configuration mode), it would be:\\n##\\nactivate interfaces ge-1/0/0\\nactivate interfaces ge-2/0/0\\n##\\n\\nNote: The Juniper equivalent of Cisco\\'s \"no shutdown\" is to set the interface\\'s disable parameter to \"false\" in configuration mode, or use the \"activate\" command in operational mode. I\\'ve provided both versions for clarity, but the configuration mode version (set disable false) is the direct counterpart to Cisco\\'s configuration command.', 'trans_template': 'activate interfaces ge-1/0/0'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'activate interfaces ge-2/0/0', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 disable false\\nset interfaces ge-2/0/0 disable false\\n##\\n\\nAlternatively, if using the operational mode command (not configuration mode), it would be:\\n##\\nactivate interfaces ge-1/0/0\\nactivate interfaces ge-2/0/0\\n##\\n\\nNote: The Juniper equivalent of Cisco\\'s \"no shutdown\" is to set the interface\\'s disable parameter to \"false\" in configuration mode, or use the \"activate\" command in operational mode. I\\'ve provided both versions for clarity, but the configuration mode version (set disable false) is the direct counterpart to Cisco\\'s configuration command.', 'trans_template': 'activate interfaces ge-2/0/0'}]]": 1,
    "['switchport trunk allowed vlan [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family ethernet-switching vlan members 10', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco command:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family ethernet-switching vlan members 10\\nset interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family ethernet-switching vlan members 10\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper:\\n1. The interface naming is different (ge-1/0/0 instead of GigabitEthernet1/0/0)\\n2. We need to specify the unit and family for Ethernet switching\\n3. The \"vlan members\" command is Juniper\\'s equivalent to Cisco\\'s \"switchport trunk allowed vlan\"\\n4. Both interfaces need separate commands as Juniper doesn\\'t have interface range capability in the same way as Cisco', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family ethernet-switching vlan members 10'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family ethernet-switching vlan members 10', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco command:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family ethernet-switching vlan members 10\\nset interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family ethernet-switching vlan members 10\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper:\\n1. The interface naming is different (ge-1/0/0 instead of GigabitEthernet1/0/0)\\n2. We need to specify the unit and family for Ethernet switching\\n3. The \"vlan members\" command is Juniper\\'s equivalent to Cisco\\'s \"switchport trunk allowed vlan\"\\n4. Both interfaces need separate commands as Juniper doesn\\'t have interface range capability in the same way as Cisco', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family ethernet-switching vlan members 10'}]]": 1
